A = [
dict(n=18, slug="speculative-decoding", title="How Can a Small AI Make a Big AI Faster?", topic="Speculative decoding",
buzz='"Speculative decoding." One of the tricks behind faster AI answers. It sounds like gambling.',
hw='A fast intern drafts the next five words of a letter. The expert reads the draft and says: "first three are fine, fix the fourth." The expert checked five words in about the time it takes to write one.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a small, fast model **guesses ahead**, and the big model **checks** the guesses in one go.',
step='Writing normally means the big model produces one token at a time (write-up 3), which is slow. Checking several tokens at once is much faster for it than writing them one by one.\n\nSo the small model drafts a few tokens, the big model verifies them together, keeps the ones it agrees with, and corrects the first one it doesn\'t. The final text is what the big model would have written anyway, just faster.',
met='AI apps that suddenly got noticeably faster without changing models. Many serving systems use this or similar tricks.',
name='**Speculative decoding**.',
words=[("Draft model","the small, fast model that guesses ahead."),("Target model","the big model that checks and decides."),("Decoding","producing the output, token by token.")],
notit='It doesn\'t lower quality or change the answer; the big model still has the final say. It only helps when the small model\'s guesses are often right.',
take=["Know that speed gains don't always mean a weaker model.","Ask your provider which speed tricks they use before assuming quality changed.","Expect the biggest gains on predictable text, like code and forms."],
close="The intern drafts. The expert checks. Faster, same answer.",
q="Have you noticed your AI tools getting faster lately?",
scene='An expert writes one word at a time. Slow.\nAn intern drafts five words ahead. The expert just checks.',
hook="That's speculative decoding: guess ahead, check in one go.",
extra="Same answer, faster."),

dict(n=19, slug="gpus", title="Why Does AI Run on Gaming Chips?", topic="Why AI needs GPUs",
buzz='"We\'re short of GPUs." Chip makers became some of the world\'s most valuable companies. Why does AI need graphics chips?',
hw='You need to solve a million simple sums. One brilliant professor does them one at a time. A thousand students each do a thousand, all at once.\n\nThe students finish far sooner. That\'s the hello world: a **CPU** is the professor, a **GPU** is the room full of students. AI is mostly a mountain of simple sums.',
step='Every token an AI reads or writes means multiplying huge grids of numbers, its knobs (write-up 8), many times over. Each sum is easy, but there are billions, and most can be done at the same time.\n\nGPUs were designed to colour millions of screen pixels at once for games, which is exactly this kind of work. AI chips today add lots of fast memory too, because the model\'s knobs have to sit right next to the sums.',
met='News about Nvidia, chip shortages and AI data centres. Gaming PCs that can also run AI models locally.',
name='**GPUs** (graphics processing units), and **parallel computing**.',
words=[("CPU","a general-purpose chip, great at one complex task at a time."),("GPU","a chip with thousands of small cores doing simple sums in parallel."),("Parallel computing","doing many calculations at the same time.")],
notit='A GPU isn\'t smarter than a CPU. It\'s built for many simple sums at once. Other AI chips (like Google\'s TPUs) use the same idea.',
take=["Read \"GPU shortage\" as \"not enough rooms full of students\".","Remember memory matters as much as speed for running big models.","For small models, a normal laptop can be enough."],
close="One professor or a thousand students? AI needs the students.",
q="Did you know AI chips started as gaming chips?",
scene='A million simple sums.\nOne professor, or a thousand students working at once?',
hook="AI picks the students. That's why it runs on GPUs.",
extra="AI is mostly a mountain of simple sums."),

dict(n=20, slug="on-device-ai", title="Can an AI Live on Your Phone?", topic="Small and on-device models",
buzz='"On-device AI." Phone makers now advertise AI that runs without the internet. How does something that needed data centres fit in your pocket?',
hw='A library has every book, but you have to travel there. A pocket dictionary is small, always with you, and fine for most quick look-ups.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a **small language model** on your phone is the pocket dictionary; the big cloud AI is the library.',
step='Small models have a few billion knobs (write-up 8) instead of hundreds of billions. They\'re made capable through good data and distillation (write-up 12), and shrunk further with quantization (write-up 15). Phones now include chips built for this.\n\nThe benefits: works offline, answers instantly, and your data can stay on the phone. The trade-off: less knowledge and weaker reasoning, so many phones send hard questions to the cloud.',
met='Phone features that summarise notifications, clean up photos or transcribe calls without an internet connection.',
name='**Small language models (SLMs)** and **on-device AI**.',
words=[("SLM","a language model small enough for phones or laptops."),("On-device","running on your own device, not in the cloud."),("Edge AI","AI running close to where data is created.")],
notit='On-device doesn\'t always mean private. Check whether a feature quietly hands harder requests to the cloud.',
take=["Use on-device AI for quick, private, everyday tasks.","Check which features stay on the phone and which go to the cloud.","Expect a mix: pocket dictionary first, library when needed."],
close="A pocket dictionary for quick look-ups, the library for the hard ones.",
q="Which AI feature on your phone works offline?",
scene='The library has every book, but it\'s across town.\nThe pocket dictionary is in your hand.',
hook="On-device AI is the pocket dictionary.",
extra="Small, fast, private, and good enough for most quick jobs."),

dict(n=21, slug="reasoning-models", title="Why Does the AI Think Before It Answers Now?", topic="Reasoning models",
buzz='"Reasoning models." "Thinking mode." Newer AIs pause, show "thinking...", then answer. What\'s happening in that pause?',
hw='Ask a friend: "A bat and a ball cost ₹110. The bat costs ₹100 more than the ball. How much is the ball?" Most blurt "₹10". Wrong. Ask them to work it out step by step, and they get ₹5.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a **reasoning model** writes out its working before giving the answer.',
step='Normal next-token prediction (write-up 3) commits to an answer straight away. A reasoning model is trained to first produce a long chain of steps: try an approach, check it, backtrack, try again. Only then does it answer.\n\nMore thinking usually means better results on maths, coding and planning, but it uses more tokens, so it\'s slower and costs more per question.',
met='"Thinking" or "extended thinking" toggles in chat apps, and answers that take 20 seconds instead of 2.',
name='**Reasoning models**, using **test-time compute**.',
words=[("Chain of thought","the step-by-step working the model writes out."),("Test-time compute","spending more computing power while answering, not while training."),("Thinking budget","a limit on how long it can think.")],
notit='The thinking isn\'t a perfect window into how it decided, and more thinking isn\'t always better. Simple questions don\'t need it, and you pay for every thinking token.',
take=["Turn thinking on for maths, code and planning; off for quick facts.","Watch the cost: thinking tokens are billed.","Still check the final answer, not just the working."],
close="Work it out, then answer.",
q="Did you get ₹10 or ₹5?",
scene='A bat and a ball cost ₹110. The bat costs ₹100 more.\nHow much is the ball?',
hook="Most people blurt ₹10. Thinking step by step gets ₹5. Reasoning models do the same.",
extra="They write out their working before answering."),

dict(n=22, slug="multimodal", title="How Can One AI Read, See and Listen?", topic="Multimodal AI",
buzz='"Multimodal." Every new model announcement says it. It means more than "has a camera button".',
hw='Show an AI a photo of your fridge and ask: "What can I cook tonight?" It names the ingredients and suggests a recipe.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: one AI that takes in **text, images and sound** together and reasons across them.',
step='Remember tokens (write-up 2) and embeddings (write-up 4)? Images are cut into small patches, and sound into short slices. Each patch or slice is turned into the same kind of numbers as words, on the same meaning map.\n\nOnce everything is on one map, the model can link "red round thing in the photo" to the word "tomato" and to "salad".',
met='Pointing your phone at a sign to translate it. Asking an AI about a chart or a screenshot. Talking to an assistant that can also see your camera.',
name='**Multimodal AI**.',
words=[("Modality","a type of input or output: text, image, audio, video."),("Image patch","a small square of a picture, treated like a token."),("Vision-language model","a model that understands images and text together.")],
notit='Seeing isn\'t understanding perfectly. Multimodal models still misread small print, count objects badly and miss details. Check what matters.',
take=["Try sending a photo or chart instead of describing it.","Double-check numbers it reads from images.","Ask which inputs a model really supports: text, images, audio, video?"],
close="Words, pictures and sounds, all on one map of meaning.",
q="What's the most useful thing you've shown an AI a photo of?",
scene='A photo of your fridge.\n"What can I cook tonight?"',
hook="One AI that reads, sees and listens. That's multimodal.",
extra="Pictures and sounds become tokens too."),

dict(n=23, slug="diffusion", title="How Does an AI Draw a Picture From Noise?", topic="Diffusion",
buzz='"Diffusion models." The tech behind most AI image and video generators. It sounds like a chemistry lesson.',
hw='Take a clear photo of a cat. Add a little static. Then more. Then more, until it\'s pure TV snow.\n\nNow teach an AI to go **backwards**: remove a little static at a time. That\'s the hello world: a **diffusion model** starts from noise and cleans it, step by step, into a picture.',
step='During training, the AI sees millions of images being turned into noise and learns to predict the noise so it can remove it.\n\nTo create something new, it starts from fresh random noise and repeats the cleaning dozens of times. Your text prompt, "a cat in a spacesuit", steers each step, so the picture that emerges matches the words.',
met='AI image generators and the newer AI video tools. "Remove background" and "fill in this area" photo features often use the same idea.',
name='**Diffusion models**.',
words=[("Noise","random static, like a detuned TV."),("Denoising","removing noise a little at a time."),("Prompt guidance","the text steering each cleaning step.")],
notit='The AI isn\'t searching for an existing picture and copying it. But it learned from existing images, which is why copyright and artist consent are real debates.',
take=["Write prompts that describe what you want to see, not instructions.","Check hands, text and small details; they still go wrong.","Know your company's rules on AI-made images and their sources."],
close="Start with static. Clean it, step by step, into a picture.",
q="Have you made an AI image you were proud of?",
scene='A cat photo, covered in more and more static,\nuntil it\'s pure TV snow.',
hook="Now run it backwards. That's how a diffusion model draws.",
extra="Noise, cleaned step by step into a picture."),

dict(n=24, slug="voice-ai", title="How Does an AI Talk Back Without a Pause?", topic="Voice AI",
buzz='"Real-time voice AI." Assistants that chat like a person, even when you interrupt. How did talking to computers stop feeling like leaving voicemails?',
hw='Old voice assistants were a relay race: one system turned your speech into text, another wrote an answer, a third read it aloud. Each hand-off added a pause.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: newer **voice AI** handles hearing, thinking and speaking in one model, so it can reply almost instantly, and even notice when you interrupt.',
step='The relay approach (speech-to-text, then text AI, then text-to-speech) still works and is easy to control. But it loses tone, like a sarcastic "great", and adds delay.\n\nSpeech-to-speech models turn audio into tokens (write-up 2) directly, reason over them, and produce audio tokens back. They can hear emotion, pause naturally and stop when you talk over them.',
met='Voice modes in chat apps that let you interrupt mid-sentence. Customer service bots that sound surprisingly human.',
name='**Voice AI**: **speech-to-speech** models, versus the **ASR, LLM, TTS** pipeline.',
words=[("ASR","automatic speech recognition: speech to text."),("TTS","text-to-speech: text to spoken audio."),("Latency","the delay before the reply starts.")],
notit='A natural voice isn\'t a sign the AI is more correct. And a human-sounding voice should still tell people they\'re talking to an AI.',
take=["Test voice bots with interruptions and background noise.","Tell callers when they're speaking to an AI.","Measure the delay; anything over a second feels slow."],
close="Hear, think and speak in one breath.",
q="Have you ever been surprised a voice was an AI?",
scene='Old voice assistants: speech to text, text to answer, answer to speech.\nThree hand-offs. Three pauses.',
hook="New voice AI hears, thinks and speaks in one breath.",
extra="So you can interrupt it, like a person."),

dict(n=25, slug="open-weight", title="What Does \"Open\" Mean in Open AI Models?", topic="Open-weight vs closed models",
buzz='"Open-source AI." "Open-weight model." "Closed model." The words get mixed up in every debate. They don\'t mean the same thing.',
hw='A restaurant dish: you can eat it, but only at the restaurant, and you never see the recipe. That\'s a **closed** model, used through an app or API.\n\nA recipe you take home: you cook it in your own kitchen, adjust it, and keep it. That\'s an **open-weight** model: you download its knobs (write-up 8) and run it yourself.',
step='"Open weights" means the trained model file is shared, often with a licence that has conditions. It usually doesn\'t mean the training data or full training method is shared; that would be fully "open source" in the strict sense, which is rarer.\n\nOpen-weight models give control, privacy and no per-question fee, but you run the servers. Closed models are easier and often more capable, but you depend on the provider.',
met='Model families like Llama, Mistral, Qwen or DeepSeek that you can download, versus services you can only use through an API.',
name='**Open-weight** versus **closed** models.',
words=[("Weights","the trained knobs; the model itself."),("Licence","the rules for how you may use the weights."),("Self-hosting","running the model on your own servers.")],
notit='Open-weight isn\'t automatically "free for anything". Read the licence. And it isn\'t automatically safer or less safe; that depends on how you run it.',
take=["Check the licence before building on an open-weight model.","Compare total cost: servers and people, not just the free download.","Use open weights where data must stay in your own systems."],
close="Eat at the restaurant, or take the recipe home.",
q="Restaurant or home kitchen: which does your team use?",
scene='A restaurant dish: you eat it, but never see the recipe.\nA recipe card: you cook it at home, your way.',
hook="That's the difference between closed and open-weight AI models.",
extra="\"Open\" usually means the recipe, not the ingredients list."),
]
